NAME
    disasm - disassemble instructions from memory or a processor code space

SYNOPSIS
    disasm <space> <addr> [<n>] [m8|m16] [x8|x16]
    disasm <cpu> [<addr>] [<n>] [m8|m16] [x8|x16]

DESCRIPTION
    Linearly disassembles <n> instructions (default 10) starting at hex
    <addr>, printing address, raw bytes, mnemonic, operands, and any
    matching labels from label(1).

    Naming a processor from cpus(1) selects that processor's code space
    and instruction set (for example, 65816 for SNES main CPU and SA-1,
    SPC700 for sound, GSU RISC, or 24-bit NEC DSP words). When <addr> is
    omitted in the processor form, disassembly starts at that processor's
    current program counter. Naming a raw memory space selects the
    disassembler associated with that space (for example, APURAM decodes
    as SPC700).

    For NEC DSP program space (DSPPRG), <addr> is a program word index
    matching the DSP's PC, while mem(1) reads DSPPRG as raw bytes (3 bytes
    per word). Because disassembly is linear, embedded data tables may
    decode as arbitrary instructions until the stream resynchronizes.

OPTIONS
    m8 | m16
        65816 only: force the starting accumulator/memory operand width to
        8-bit or 16-bit instead of reading the live M flag. Requesting m16
        selects native mode so emulation mode does not override the width.

    x8 | x16
        65816 only: force the starting index register operand width to
        8-bit or 16-bit instead of reading the live X flag. Requesting x16
        selects native mode.

    Width flags may appear in any position after 'disasm' and do not count
    as <addr> or <n>. REP and SEP instructions encountered inside the
    disassembled range update widths forward automatically, and the
    starting widths are printed above the listing.

EXAMPLES
    disasm CpuBus 8000 20
    disasm gsu
    disasm spc 05A5 10
    disasm dsp 0 20
    disasm cpu 04FDD8 12 m16 x8

EXIT STATUS
    0   Disassembly printed (or usage/unknown-target message printed).
    1   No target attached, invalid hex argument, or session service fault.

SEE ALSO
    cpus(1), cov(1), bt(1), label(1), callers(1), mem(1)
